# Lab gateway. Terminates TLS for internal service names and proxies to the
# services behind it. See docs/gateway.md.
#
# Certificates are real Let's Encrypt certificates obtained with the DNS-01
# challenge against the labxp.io zone. DNS-01 proves control of a name by
# writing a TXT record rather than by answering an inbound request, which is
# why every name below works in a browser with a normal padlock while the lab
# accepts no inbound connection from the internet and no internal CA root is
# installed on any device.
#
# TO ADD A SERVICE: copy a block, point it at the upstream, add the matching
# A record to BOTH Pi-holes (192.168.10.11 and .12) pointing at
# 192.168.201.14, and open the firewall from pfe to the upstream. Then push --
# gateway-sync picks it up within the minute. No Terraform, no VM rebuild.
{
	# Recommended, currently unset: an ACME account with no contact address
	# gets no expiry or account warnings from Let's Encrypt. Add one line:
	#   email you@example.com
	# Left out rather than guessed; a wrong address is worse than none.

	# Staging CA for testing. Let's Encrypt allows 5 duplicate certificates
	# per week, and a loop that keeps failing after issuance burns that fast.
	# Uncomment while debugging issuance, then remove -- staging certs are
	# not trusted by browsers.
	# acme_ca https://acme-staging-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory
}

# OpenClaw refuses a proxied request unless it can attribute the client:
#   "Proxy client attribution is required. Configure gateway.trustedProxies
#    narrowly and make the proxy overwrite or safely rebuild forwarded client
#    headers."
#
# Caddy APPENDS to X-Forwarded-For by default, preserving whatever the client
# sent. That is the right default on the open internet, where an upstream
# proxy's entry matters -- and exactly what OpenClaw rejects, because an
# attacker-supplied X-Forwarded-For would survive into the header the gateway
# reads. Overwriting it with the peer address Caddy actually accepted the
# connection from is the "overwrite or safely rebuild" the error asks for.
#
# This is only half the fix. OpenClaw must also list this gateway in
# gateway.trustedProxies (192.168.201.14) or it has no reason to believe the
# header either.
(openclaw_upstream) {
	reverse_proxy 192.168.200.37:{args[0]} {
		header_up X-Forwarded-For {http.request.remote.host}
	}
}

# Shared TLS configuration. Every site below imports it.
(tls_cloudflare) {
	tls {
		dns cloudflare {env.CF_DNS_API_TOKEN}

		# Pin propagation checks to public resolvers. Caddy confirms its
		# _acme-challenge TXT record is visible before asking Let's Encrypt
		# to validate; left on the system resolver that check would go to
		# Pi-hole, which holds local overrides for these very names, and a
		# split-horizon answer makes the check fail or hang.
		resolvers 1.1.1.1 9.9.9.9
	}
}

# --- The portal -------------------------------------------------------------
# The apex, not a subdomain. That is a deliberate choice with one consequence
# worth knowing: both Pi-holes answer labxp.io with this host's address, so
# anything published at the apex on the public internet is unreachable from
# inside the lab. Subdomains are unaffected -- cmd.labxp.io still resolves
# publicly -- PROVIDED the Pi-hole entries are plain host records for the
# exact name. See gateway/README.md; a dnsmasq `address=/labxp.io/...` line
# would match every subdomain and take cmd.labxp.io down with it.
labxp.io {
	import tls_cloudflare
	reverse_proxy homepage:3000
}

# --- Private frontends ------------------------------------------------------
redlib.labxp.io {
	import tls_cloudflare
	reverse_proxy redlib:8080
}

plex.labxp.io {
	import tls_cloudflare

	# HTTPS upstream, even though Plex also answers plain HTTP on the same
	# port, and the reason is not security -- it is that Plex builds redirects
	# from the scheme of the connection it received, ignoring
	# X-Forwarded-Proto. Proxied over HTTP it answered /web with
	#   302 -> http://plex.labxp.io/web/index.html
	# downgrading the browser to plain HTTP mid-navigation. The redirect chain
	# breaks and Plex's client falls back to app.plex.tv, which looks like the
	# gateway not working at all rather than like a scheme bug.
	#
	# Over HTTPS the same request answers https://.../web/index.html, so the
	# scheme survives and no header rewriting is needed.
	#
	# The certificate Plex presents is issued for *.plex.direct and cannot
	# match an IP address, so this hop cannot be verified. It stays inside the
	# lab between two hosts it owns; the browser's hop keeps its real
	# Let's Encrypt certificate.
	reverse_proxy https://192.168.10.10:32400 {
		# Send the browser's hostname, not the upstream's. Naming the upstream
		# with a scheme makes Caddy rewrite Host to the dial address, and Plex
		# builds redirects from Host -- so /web answered
		#   302 -> https://192.168.10.10:32400/web/index.html
		# which is correct HTTPS and still useless: it sends the browser at an
		# address that bypasses the gateway, has no matching certificate, and is
		# unreachable from any VLAN but Plex's own.
		#
		# Verified against Plex directly: the same request with
		# `Host: plex.labxp.io` answers https://plex.labxp.io/web/index.html.
		header_up Host {host}

		transport http {
			tls_insecure_skip_verify
		}
	}
}

# --- Infrastructure ---------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately NOT pve.labxp.io. That name is the Proxmox API endpoint the
# bpg/proxmox provider talks to from CI; repointing it would put every
# Terraform run in this repository through Caddy, and a proxy hiccup would
# become a CI outage across every deployment. This is a second name for
# browser access only.
#
# Proxmox serves its own self-signed certificate on 8006, so the hop from
# Caddy to it cannot be verified. That hop stays inside the lab; the hop the
# browser makes is the one that is properly secured.
proxmox.labxp.io {
	import tls_cloudflare
	reverse_proxy https://pve.labxp.io:8006 {
		transport http {
			tls_insecure_skip_verify
		}
	}
}

# Pi-hole's UI lives under /admin and its root serves a blocked-domain page,
# so send the bare hostname there rather than making everyone remember the
# path. Both are proxied separately on purpose: reaching one when the other
# is rebooting is the entire reason there are two.
#
# HTTPS upstream, not plain HTTP. Pi-hole serves TLS itself and answers :80
# with a 308 to the HTTPS URL, so proxying to :80 produced a redirect loop
# dressed up as a working page -- Caddy returned the 308 to the browser,
# which came back to Caddy, which asked Pi-hole again.
#
# The certificate it presents is self-signed and carries its own name, not
# dns01.labxp.io, so this hop cannot be verified and skipping verification is
# the only option short of trusting Pi-hole's CA on the gateway. That hop
# stays inside the lab between two hosts it owns; the hop the browser makes,
# which is the one worth protecting, is properly secured.
dns01.labxp.io {
	import tls_cloudflare
	redir / /admin
	reverse_proxy https://192.168.10.11 {
		transport http {
			tls_insecure_skip_verify
		}
	}
}

dns02.labxp.io {
	import tls_cloudflare
	redir / /admin
	reverse_proxy https://192.168.10.12 {
		transport http {
			tls_insecure_skip_verify
		}
	}
}

# --- Home automation --------------------------------------------------------
# hom01, not home: home.labxp.io is the WAN VPN endpoint and taking that name
# internally would black-hole the VPN for anyone inside the lab -- the one
# name you would want working while diagnosing why you cannot get in.
#
# Home Assistant is websocket-heavy (the whole UI updates over one), which
# Caddy proxies transparently. It also refuses requests from an unknown proxy
# unless told to expect one: set http.trusted_proxies in configuration.yaml to
# 192.168.201.14 or the UI loads and then sits there failing to connect.
hom01.labxp.io {
	import tls_cloudflare
	reverse_proxy 192.168.1.15:8123
}

# --- OpenClaw ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Two Gateway instances on one host, separated by port: 18789 is the default
# profile, 19789 the `work` profile.
#
# THE ADDRESS IS A DHCP LEASE, NOT A RESERVATION. openclaw-2's static
# addressing is staged in terraform/deployments/lab but still commented out,
# waiting on four VLAN 200 facts. This is the exact failure that work exists
# to prevent: a rebuild once moved this host from .36 to .37, and everything
# naming the old address quietly pointed at nothing. Two portal links and two
# firewall rules now name .37, so a rebuild breaks all four at once and none
# of them will say why. Pin the address when those facts are available.
orca.labxp.io {
	import tls_cloudflare
	import openclaw_upstream 18789
}

workai.labxp.io {
	import tls_cloudflare
	import openclaw_upstream 19789
}
